# Villa Almerico Capra, called "La Rotonda" — Andrea Palladio, begun 1567 — the piano nobile.
#
# Palladio built this one to be indifferent to which way you look at it: a square block,
# a domed circular hall dead centre, and FOUR identical temple fronts, one per face. Turn
# the drawing a quarter turn and it is the same drawing. So the source is written the same
# way — ONE quarter, authored once, placed four times:
#
#   place quarter() as north at (ORG, 0)
#   place quarter() as east  at (EXT, ORG)       rotate 90
#   place quarter() as south at (EXT - ORG, EXT) rotate 180
#   place quarter() as west  at (0, EXT - ORG)   rotate 270
#
# Everything below the `component` line is written once and drawn four times. The symmetry
# is not asserted, checked or eyeballed — it is the only thing that CAN come out, because
# `place`'s quarter-turn is an exact signed-permutation matrix (entries -1|0|1, |det| = 1),
# so the four porticos are congruent to the millimetre by construction.
#
# WHY THE QUARTER IS A PINWHEEL AND NOT A QUARTER-SQUARE. The obvious cut — slice the
# square along both centre lines and place the top-left corner four times — severs the
# axial corridor down the middle, so every corridor would have to be authored as two
# halves in two different instances, with the entrance door on the seam. Cut the ring of
# rooms into four congruent STRIPS instead, each running the full block width on one side
# and stopping short on the other:
#
#     +---------------------------+          the north strip runs x 0..18000
#     |   north strip        |    |          the east strip is that strip, turned 90 deg,
#     |----------------+     |    |          and so on round the square: a pinwheel.
#     |   |            |     |    |          Four strips tile the ring exactly, and not
#     |   |   (the centre     ) e |          one room, wall or door is cut in half.
#     | w |    band, with     | a |
#     |   |   the rotonda     ) s |
#     |   |                   | t |
#     |    -----+-------------+---|
#     |         |    south strip  |
#     +---------------------------+
#
# The consequence worth reading twice is that each strip declares only the walls it owns,
# and the FOUR COPIES INTERLOCK. The block's north face, for instance, is the north
# strip's own shell run; the wall closing the north side of the centre band is three
# different statements — the west strip's trailing wall, the north strip's band wall, and
# the east strip's corner wall — each landing on y = 19000 after its own rotation. Nothing
# in the source says so. The pinwheel does.
#
# THE IDEAL SQUARE. 25 x 25 m centreline to centreline, in bands of 7 | 11 | 7. The middle
# band is 11 m square and the rotonda is inscribed in it, so the drum is TANGENT to all
# four centre-band walls and every tangent point is a threshold — which is why each of the
# four thresholds is cut twice, once through the straight wall and once through the arc
# (`o_band` and `o_drum`, both in the component). Miss the second cut and the poche of the
# other wall still stands in the doorway. The four corners the circle leaves inside the
# square belong to no room and carry no schedule row: they are the masonry the dome stands
# on, which is exactly what they are in Vicenza.
#
# EVEN THE DRUM IS QUARTERED. The rotonda is not an exception to the pinwheel: each
# quarter carries 90 degrees of it, as two arcs, and four of those close one circle. Only
# the FLOOR is authored at the centre. Doing it that way makes the circle's segmentation
# four-fold as well as its shape — see the note on `drum` below for why the obvious
# construction cannot be, and why two arcs per quarter rather than one (an analysis grid
# rasterises a wall between its VERTICES, so a long chord lays a phantom wall across the
# hall). The drawing is identical either way: every face is a true SVG/DXF arc at any zoom.
#
# ONLY WALLS, ROOMS AND OPENINGS SURVIVE A QUARTER TURN EXACTLY, which is why the columns
# here are round wall stubs and the steps are their own tread lines rather than the
# `column` and `stair` elements. Both element notes are at their use sites, and the reason
# is in README.md.
#
# LIBERTIES, stated plainly. The four service stairs are idealised to one per quarter (the
# building has two, and they spiral); the interior is drawn at a tidy 25 m square where the
# real block is nearer 26.4 m; and the real villa is turned 45 degrees on its hill so that
# every facade takes sun, which a plan drawn square to the page cannot show. See README.md.
plan "Villa La Rotonda — Piano Nobile" {
  units mm
  grid 50
  paper A1 portrait
  scale 1:100
  north up
  schedule rooms
  legend

  # Sepia ink on laid paper — one hex value becomes the whole palette.
  theme from "#5b4636"

  # ---- the ideal square ------------------------------------------------------
  let A     = 7000              # corner band
  let B     = 11000             # centre band — and the rotonda's diameter
  let SIDE  = 2 * A + B         # 25000, the block, centreline to centreline
  let STRIP = A + B             # 18000, how far one pinwheel strip reaches
  let R     = B / 2             # 5500, the drum's centreline radius

  let PORT  = 5000              # portico platform, front columns to shell
  let STEPS = 7000              # the flight in front of it
  let PROJ  = PORT + STEPS      # 12000, how far a portico reaches past the block

  let ORG   = PROJ              # the block's top-left corner, in plan coordinates
  let EXT   = SIDE + 2 * PROJ   # 49000 overall, both ways — the drawing is square
  let CX    = EXT / 2           # 24500, the centre of the hall, of the block, of everything

  # The nine-square, as structural datum lines.
  axes {
    x at ORG, ORG + A, ORG + A + B, ORG + SIDE
    y at ORG, ORG + A, ORG + A + B, ORG + SIDE
  }

  # ---- ONE QUARTER -----------------------------------------------------------
  # Authored in its own coordinates, from (0,0) at the outer corner of the steps. Local y
  # runs INWARD: 0 is the bottom step, FACE is the outer wall, BACK is the centre band.
  # A `place` carries all of it — walls, arcs, room boxes, hinges and door swings — through
  # one rigid transform, so nothing in here knows or cares which of the four it becomes.
  component quarter() {
    let AXIS = SIDE / 2         # 12500, the portico's centre line in local x
    let FACE = PROJ             # 12000, local y of the block's outer wall
    let BACK = PROJ + A         # 19000, local y of the centre band
    let SALA = 5000             # depth of the corner room; the camerino takes the rest
    let CORR = 4000             # the corridor's clear width
    let CW   = AXIS - CORR / 2  # 10500, corridor west face
    let CE   = AXIS + CORR / 2  # 14500, corridor east face

    # The shell run this strip owns: up the side, then along the whole face. Four of
    # these, turned, close the square. Openings are positioned along the RUN, which
    # crosses the corner without a break — the face starts at run = A.
    wall id=shell exterior thickness 500 material brick {
      (0, BACK) (0, FACE) (STRIP, FACE)
    }

    # The centre band's edge, from the corner square across to the strip's end. Its
    # midpoint is the tangent point where the drum touches — hence the 50% below.
    wall id=w_band partition thickness 300 { (A, BACK) (STRIP, BACK) }

    # THIS QUARTER'S 90 DEGREES OF THE DRUM. The rotonda is not an exception to the
    # pinwheel; it is quartered like everything else, and four of these close the circle.
    #
    # Writing it here rather than once at the centre is what makes the circle's
    # SEGMENTATION four-fold too. The obvious construction — four walls each symmetric
    # about its own tangent point — needs split points at 45 degrees, and 5500/sqrt(2) is
    # not a number any grid can hold; snap it and the drum stops being one circle. Split
    # instead on the 3-4-5 triple (3300, 4400), which is exactly on R, and let `place`
    # supply the symmetry: this wall spans 36.87 degrees before its tangent point and
    # 53.13 after, and the next quarter's is the same wall turned, so the eight arcs come
    # out in the same order whichever way you turn the page.
    #
    # OY is the centre of the rotonda in this quarter's own coordinates. BACK = OY - R is
    # the tangent point, which is why the centre band's edge is tangent by construction
    # and not by arithmetic.
    # The run goes EAST TO WEST across the top of the circle, not west to east, and the
    # direction is load-bearing: an `arc` bulges to the LEFT of travel, so walking it the
    # other way puts the centre on the wrong side and the compiler draws the other circle
    # through the same two points — four petals instead of a drum. Walked this way the
    # default needs no `cw`.
    let OY = BACK + R
    wall id=drum partition thickness 600 material brick {
      (AXIS + 3300, OY - 4400) arc (AXIS, BACK) radius R arc (AXIS - 4400, OY - 3300) radius R
    }

    wall id=w_corner partition thickness 300 { (A, FACE)     (A, BACK) }
    wall id=w_cw     partition thickness 300 { (CW, FACE)    (CW, BACK) }
    wall id=w_ce     partition thickness 300 { (CE, FACE)    (CE, BACK) }
    # The trailing wall: this strip's far end, which after the quarter-turn becomes the
    # NEXT quarter's party wall. The one door through it closes the circuit of rooms.
    wall id=w_trail  partition thickness 300 { (STRIP, FACE) (STRIP, BACK) }
    wall id=w_cam    partition thickness 200 { (0, FACE + SALA) (A, FACE + SALA) }

    # ---- rooms ---------------------------------------------------------------
    room id=loggia    at (A, STEPS)       size B x PORT           label "Loggia"    uses entry
    room id=corridoio at (CW, FACE)       size CORR x A           label "Corridoio" uses hall circulation
    room id=sala      at (0, FACE)        size A x SALA           label "Sala"      uses living
    room id=camerino  at (0, FACE + SALA) size A x (A - SALA)     label "Camerino"  uses storage
    room id=scaletta  at (A, FACE)        size (CW - A) x A       label "Scaletta"  uses circulation
    room id=camera    at (CE, FACE)       size (STRIP - CE) x A   label "Camera"    uses bedroom

    # ---- the temple front ----------------------------------------------------
    # Hexastyle: six columns across, on 1800 bays with the middle one widened to 2500 so
    # the door keeps its own axis, and one more returning down each flank — which is what
    # makes it a portico and not a screen.
    #
    # A column here is a WALL, not the `column` element, and the reason is the whole point
    # of this drawing: only walls, rooms and openings survive a quarter-turn exactly. See
    # README.md — the instance transform reads a column's `at` as a centre where the
    # element defines it as a top-left, so a placed colonnade walks 800 mm off its own
    # axis on two of the four faces. Symmetry you can SEE is worth an element you cannot
    # use.
    #
    # And a wall makes a ROUND column, which a square element could not. A closed run of
    # two arcs is a circle on the wall's CENTRELINE, and its faces land at that radius
    # PLUS AND MINUS half the thickness — so a centreline of COL_R / 2 carrying a
    # thickness of COL_R has an outer face at COL_R and an inner face at nothing at all.
    # The annulus closes into a solid disc, filled with the same poche a plan-cut column
    # carries anyway.
    let COL_R = 400                 # the column as drawn: 800 diameter
    let CL    = COL_R / 2           # the centreline circle it is built from
    let CY    = STEPS + COL_R       # the front row stands on the platform's outer edge
    let colx = [AXIS - 4850, AXIS - 3050, AXIS - 1250, AXIS + 1250, AXIS + 3050, AXIS + 4850]
    for i in 0..6 {
      wall partition thickness COL_R {
        (colx[i] + CL, CY) arc (colx[i] - CL, CY) radius CL arc (colx[i] + CL, CY) radius CL
      }
    }
    wall partition thickness COL_R {
      (colx[0] + CL, CY + 1800) arc (colx[0] - CL, CY + 1800) radius CL arc (colx[0] + CL, CY + 1800) radius CL
    }
    wall partition thickness COL_R {
      (colx[5] + CL, CY + 1800) arc (colx[5] - CL, CY + 1800) radius CL arc (colx[5] + CL, CY + 1800) radius CL
    }

    # The flight down to the garden, drawn the way a treatise draws it: the tread lines
    # themselves, between two thin edges, and no UP/DN arrow — a plate has none, and the
    # `stair` element's arrow is the other thing that does not turn with its quarter. The
    # top tread is the loggia's own floor edge, so the treads stop one riser short: that
    # keeps the flight out of the building's measured extents and the overall chain on a
    # round 35000.
    let TREAD = 700
    for i in 0..10 {
      wall partition thickness 50 { (AXIS - 3000, i * TREAD) (AXIS + 3000, i * TREAD) }
    }
    wall partition thickness 50 { (AXIS - 3000, 0) (AXIS - 3000, STEPS - TREAD) }
    wall partition thickness 50 { (AXIS + 3000, 0) (AXIS + 3000, STEPS - TREAD) }

    # ---- thresholds ----------------------------------------------------------
    # The front door, on the axis. `on <wall> at <pos>` takes a literal run position, not
    # an expression, so every number below is written out: the run starts at the strip's
    # inner corner, climbs the side wall for A = 7000, turns, and then measures local x
    # along the face. The axis is therefore 7000 + 12500 = 19500.
    door id=d_main on shell at 19500 width 2200 swing into corridoio

    # Into the rotonda. A tangent point lies on TWO walls at once, so it has to be cut
    # twice or the poche of the other one still stands in the doorway: once through the
    # straight centre-band edge, at its midpoint, and once through the arc.
    #
    # 40.9666% is not a fudge — it is 36.87 / 90, the fraction of this wall's run that
    # falls before the tangent point, and `on <wall> at <pos>` walks an arc by its ARC
    # LENGTH R*theta rather than its chord. Both figures come from the same 3-4-5 triangle
    # the drum is split on. All four quarters carry the identical number, so the four
    # thresholds are congruent exactly, whatever the rounding.
    opening id=o_band on w_band at 50% width 2400
    opening id=o_drum on drum   at 40.9666% width 2400

    # The circuit: corridor -> scaletta -> sala -> camerino, corridor -> camera, and
    # camera -> the NEXT quarter's camerino through the trailing wall. Four of those and
    # the rooms close a ring around the hall.
    door id=d_scaletta on w_cw     at 1800 width 1000 swing into scaletta
    door id=d_camera   on w_ce     at 1800 width 1000 swing into camera
    door id=d_sala     on w_corner at 1800 width 1200 swing into sala
    door id=d_camerino on w_cam    at 5500 width 900  swing into camerino
    door id=d_ring     on w_trail  at 3500 width 1000 swing into camera

    # ---- glazing -------------------------------------------------------------
    # Run positions again, all measured from the strip's inner corner. Below 7000 is the
    # side wall (run 7000 - t is t millimetres in from the face); above it is the face
    # (run 7000 + x is local x).
    window id=win_sala_n1 on shell at 9350  width 1400   # face, x = 2350
    window id=win_sala_n2 on shell at 11650 width 1400   # face, x = 4650
    window id=win_sala_w  on shell at 4500  width 1400   # side, 2500 in
    window id=win_cam_w   on shell at 1000  width 900    # side, 6000 in
    # The two that look out under the portico, flanking the front door.
    window id=win_scal    on shell at 15750 width 1400   # face, x = 8750
    window id=win_camera  on shell at 23250 width 1400   # face, x = 16250

    # The service stair, drawn as treads for the same reason as the entrance flight, and
    # kept at the far end of its chamber so that no leaf sweeps one and the walk from the
    # corridor through to the sala stays clear.
    for i in 0..12 {
      wall partition thickness 50 { (A + 750, FACE + 3200 + i * 300) (A + 2750, FACE + 3200 + i * 300) }
    }
    wall partition thickness 50 { (A + 750, FACE + 3200)  (A + 750, FACE + 6500) }
    wall partition thickness 50 { (A + 2750, FACE + 3200) (A + 2750, FACE + 6500) }
  }

  # ---- the four quarters -----------------------------------------------------
  # The whole building. Each origin is a corner of the pinwheel; each quarter-turn is
  # exact, so the four are congruent to the millimetre.
  place quarter() as north at (ORG, 0)
  place quarter() as east  at (EXT, ORG)       rotate 90
  place quarter() as south at (EXT - ORG, EXT) rotate 180
  place quarter() as west  at (0, EXT - ORG)   rotate 270

  # ---- the rotonda -----------------------------------------------------------
  # The drum itself came in with the quarters, four arc-pairs closing one circle. What is
  # left is the FLOOR: one room, at the centre the quarters turn about. Its area is exact
  # pi*R^2 in closed form, never the tessellation the analysis grid uses. The name is
  # nudged off centre because the diameter call-out is drawn on the diameter.
  room id=rotonda circle at (CX, CX) radius R label "Sala Centrale" at (CX, CX + 1500) uses hall

  # ---- what a plate measures -------------------------------------------------
  # `dims auto` is deliberately OFF. Every one of the 32 columns is a circular arc, so
  # auto mode reasonably hands each of them its own R400 leader — thirty-two of them,
  # across the drawing. Palladio's own plates carry no dimensions at all; these three are
  # the ones a reader cannot get from the axes bubbles and the schedule.
  #
  # A dim's offset runs along the LEFT normal of from->to, so the endpoint ORDER is what
  # puts each line outside the building. Both chains measure the OVERALL square, corner
  # to corner of the outermost step, which is the one pair of witness lines that lands in
  # empty paper instead of crossing a portico.
  dim (0, EXT)->(EXT, EXT) offset 2500
  dim (0, 0)->(0, EXT)     offset 2500
  dim diameter rotonda

  title {
    project "Villa Almerico Capra — La Rotonda"
    drawn_by "ArchLang"
    date "2026-08-25"
  }
}
